# Code standards

Every agent that changes code follows these. `docs/architecture.md` (State, Persistence, Screens, Performance) says why and where each one is used; this file is the checklist. A reviewer reports a breach as a finding against the code.

## The React Compiler

- `src/play/compiler.test.ts` stays at zero bailouts. A component or hook it cannot compile gets no memoization, and the miss spreads to whatever depends on it.
- No `oxlint-disable` or `eslint-disable` of `react/exhaustive-deps`. An effect that must run only when X changes lists X and reads the rest through `useEffectEvent`.
- No `try/catch` or `try/finally` in a component or hook body: put it in a module-level helper.
- No default parameter that is not a plain literal (`color = t.bone`): take `color?: string` and write `color ?? t.bone` in the body. No computed object keys, and no ternary inside a `||`/`&&` chain: name it in a `const` first.
- No read of the store (`getState()`), of a mutable log or of module state during a render. Pass the value in, or subscribe with a selector. The compiler caches a call by its arguments, so such a read goes stale.
- Do not hand-write a `useMemo` or `useCallback` whose dependency list the compiler cannot keep. Let the compiler memoize.

## State and caches

- A state, a card, an effect list and their arrays are never changed after the reducer returns them. A reducer step builds a new object (`updateCard`, `updateCards`). No `.push`, `.splice`, `.sort`, `delete`, `Object.assign` or field assignment on them.
- That rule is what makes identity checks and `WeakMap` caches keyed by a state or a context sound (`memoOf` in `src/rules/interpret/constant-records.ts`). Reuse `memoOf` instead of writing another two-level `WeakMap`. Do not cache on a key that can be mutated.
- A cache is bounded or uses a `WeakMap`. Its key holds everything the result depends on. A session cache keys on the whole save and the catalog version, so an Undo, a new deal or a log edit never returns a stale entry.
- Look things up by index (`effectById`, `effectsOf`), not with `find` over the effect list on a hot path.
- An engine change leaves every golden, step log and `data/` file unchanged unless the rule changed (then `ENGINE_VERSION`, as `.cursor/agents/implementer.md` says).

## Rendering the table and screens

- Subscribe narrowly. A selector returns a primitive or a stable value, and it returns its previous result when the game (and seat) are the same objects. Never subscribe to all of `game` or `settings` to read one field. A panel subscribes itself while it is open.
- Keep what re-renders the Skia canvas small. A canvas records its whole scene on every commit.
- Feedback comes before heavy work, but never changes what it answers. A haptic plays as early as its event is known (the Sent tick on acceptance, not before `reduce`). A tick tied to an animation follows the animation clock (`useAnimatedReaction` plus `scheduleOnRN`), not a `setTimeout`.
- Heavy work runs after the first paint or once: replays deferred and cached, catalog pools cached per database, module-level parses lazy and memoized, a noise texture drawn once into a tile. Expensive work spread over frames is time-boxed (about 6 ms a frame), not one item per frame.
- Hidden screens are held by React Activity (`activityEnabled`). Their effects are torn down and run again on show, so an effect must be safe to run again. It must not reopen a session, rebuild a store or reset state the player had.
- A committed state never references a released native resource (`SkImage.dispose`). Clear the state, or fall back to the live drawing, before releasing it.
- Every timer, listener, animation (`cancelAnimation`) and frame request is cancelled on unmount. A platform listener many components read (AccessibilityInfo) is one module store read through `useSyncExternalStore`.
- An optimization must not move, cover or resize a control the player taps.

## Saving

- The save is written synchronously on every accepted intent, so a crash loses nothing. An intent or an Undo writes only the open scenario (`writeActive`, `writeStandaloneActive`). `writeSave` is for log and loadout changes.
- Work deferred out of a tap that writes durable data (a run record) is flushed before anything reads it and when the app leaves the foreground.
- List screens select the columns their rows read. They do not parse every intent.

## Making a change

- Keep the behaviour the user sees exactly: the spotlight gate, skip, Back, the haptics, the banner wait, Undo and relaunch replay. If a speed-up cannot be had without changing one, stop and ask the user. Do not work around it silently.
- Measure a speed-up before and after with a scratch script in the session scratchpad, and report both numbers. The script is not committed.
- `src/screens` imports nothing from `src/rules`. The lint zones (`pnpm lint`) decide where a new helper lives.
- Match the surrounding code: dense `//` comments that say what the code does now, sentence case, rule ids and docs cited, the same naming. A comment cites a doc by file and section heading, never by line number, and does not narrate what the code used to do. Update `docs/architecture.md` (and `docs/design-system.md`, or `docs/design-system-table.md` for the table, for anything visible) wherever the change touches what they say, following `.cursor/rules/docs-standards.mdc`.
- A hook or script an agent runs prints only what the agent needs to act on. Progress bars, banners and success chatter go behind `--verbose` (scripts log through `scripts/log.ts`); a failure prints its full output.
- `pnpm test`, `pnpm lint` and `pnpm typecheck` are green before a commit; `pnpm data:check` too when the engine or `data/` could move.

## Checking a change

Run the cheap checks often and the full suite once.

- While working: `pnpm lint` and `pnpm typecheck` take seconds, so run them after each edit that could break them. Run the tests the edit touches: `pnpm vitest run <file>`, or `pnpm test:changed` for every test that imports a file changed since the last commit. `pnpm test:fast` runs everything but the slow files (`slowFiles` in `vitest.config.mts`) when the edit is wide.
- `pnpm test:changed` and `pnpm vitest related` follow imports only. A change under `data/`, `scripts/data/` or a file a test reads at run time needs `pnpm test:fast`, then the full run.
- Once, at the end: the full `pnpm test`, and `pnpm data:check` when the engine or `data/` could move. Do not run the full suite again on an unchanged tree: a green run stands until a file changes. Fix a failure with the failing file (`pnpm vitest run <file> -t "<name>"`), then run the full suite once more.
- Report each command with its result and whether the tree changed after it ran.
- A cloud session has no phone, simulator or Maestro. On-device checks (frame timing, the look in both themes, hidden-screen behaviour, haptic feel) are never marked done there. List them under Open in `docs/phases/STATUS.md`.
